1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,162
# Atomik Investor Deck 10-Minute Rehearsal Script

2
00:00:04,112 --> 00:00:07,737
Pronunciation note: say Atomik naturally.

3
00:00:07,737 --> 00:00:10,350
Do not spell it letter by letter.

4
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:13,450
Before slide one, remember the frame.

5
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:29,275
Matt presents the deck itself: opening, market, competition, mechanism, proof posture, financial model, ask, and technical Q&A. Allison is not expected to present the technical deck; she may support buyer pain, positioning, commercialization, and feedback where comfortable.

6
00:00:29,275 --> 00:00:31,000
Slide 1.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,737
Make change the unit of compute.

8
00:00:33,737 --> 00:00:37,262
Atomik is for constrained edge and embedded teams.

9
00:00:37,262 --> 00:00:41,825
They bring one workload, one baseline, and one painful constraint.

10
00:00:41,825 --> 00:00:45,637
The live ask is a five million dollar target pre-seed.

11
00:00:45,637 --> 00:00:49,475
Final SAFE mechanics are CFO and counsel pending.

12
00:00:49,475 --> 00:00:52,725
This is a proof round, not tape-out funding.

13
00:00:52,725 --> 00:00:54,500
Slide 2.

14
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:57,225
Customers already know the pain.

15
00:00:57,225 --> 00:00:59,525
Use Allison's language.

16
00:00:59,525 --> 00:01:01,962
Batteries die too quickly.

17
00:01:01,962 --> 00:01:04,287
Hardware is maxed out.

18
00:01:04,287 --> 00:01:06,500
Systems run hot.

19
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,425
Devices are too big or too heavy.

20
00:01:09,425 --> 00:01:11,700
Costs keep increasing.

21
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,075
More performance is needed.

22
00:01:14,075 --> 00:01:24,787
Before they spend more money on bigger hardware, larger batteries, cooling, bandwidth, or redesign, Atomik checks whether a constrained state path can get more out of the system they already have.

23
00:01:24,787 --> 00:01:26,650
Slide 3.

24
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,000
Customers do not buy delta-state algebra.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,425
The customer buys evidence.

26
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:41,025
The evaluation starts with a metric: bytes moved, operations coalesced, update latency, reconstruction cost, or correctness preservation.

27
00:01:41,025 --> 00:01:47,075
Customer ROI is measured only after the workload shows reduced state movement while correctness holds.

28
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:48,925
Slide 4.

29
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:51,187
Market opportunity.

30
00:01:51,187 --> 00:01:56,350
The semiconductor backdrop is above one trillion dollars in 2026.

31
00:01:56,350 --> 00:02:00,775
Embedded systems are a hundred-billion-dollar-plus adjacent pool.

32
00:02:00,775 --> 00:02:08,575
The ten to forty million dollar entry wedge is an early path from paid evaluations, design partners, and licensing if proof converts.

33
00:02:08,575 --> 00:02:11,962
It is not a forecast, and it is not a ceiling.

34
00:02:11,962 --> 00:02:13,787
Slide 5.

35
00:02:13,787 --> 00:02:15,800
Focus markets.

36
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:23,787
The focus segments are edge AI, robotics and industrial systems, drones and remote autonomy, and smallsat or remote sensing.

37
00:02:23,787 --> 00:02:33,812
The growth trajectories are the reason investors should care, but the customer trigger is still the same: battery, heat, size, weight, latency, performance, or hardware limits.

38
00:02:33,812 --> 00:02:37,775
The target-account names are examples only, not traction.

39
00:02:37,775 --> 00:02:39,637
Slide 6.

40
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:41,887
Competitive landscape.

41
00:02:41,887 --> 00:02:57,675
The alternatives are real: NVIDIA Jetson, Qualcomm, Ambarella, Hailo, Google Coral, FPGA upgrades, Arm, Synopsys ARC, Cadence Tensilica, CEVA, Andes, compression, caching, delta sync, hand optimization, bigger batteries, and more cooling.

42
00:02:57,675 --> 00:03:01,200
Atomik is not claiming to replace the whole stack.

43
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:11,900
The defensibility wedge is proof-bound IP: Lean4-checked formal algebra, FPGA hardware validation, and a claims registry that ties formal statements to exact audited properties.

44
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:13,750
Slide 7.

45
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:16,487
Make change the unit of compute.

46
00:03:16,487 --> 00:03:20,375
The primitive is reference state plus accumulated change.

47
00:03:20,375 --> 00:03:22,612
LOAD sets the reference.

48
00:03:22,612 --> 00:03:24,825
ACCUM adds changes.

49
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:26,962
READ reconstructs.

50
00:03:26,962 --> 00:03:29,400
SWAP commits a boundary.

51
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:32,250
Do not over-explain the algebra.

52
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:33,950
Slide 8.

53
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:35,862
Fit discipline.

54
00:03:35,862 --> 00:03:39,487
Atomik has a wedge, not a universal claim.

55
00:03:39,487 --> 00:03:49,400
It fits best where updates dominate reads, changes are sparse, bandwidth or latency is expensive, power budget or hardware margin is tight, or sync and replay create overhead.

56
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,150
Slide 9.

57
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:53,062
Use cases.

58
00:03:53,062 --> 00:04:02,287
Potential customers include robotics, edge AI, industrial systems, remote systems such as drones and satellites, and defense-adjacent environments.

59
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:05,687
What they share is expensive constraint pressure.

60
00:04:05,687 --> 00:04:07,462
Slide 10.

61
00:04:07,462 --> 00:04:09,212
Live proof.

62
00:04:09,212 --> 00:04:13,287
This is the current Zynq hardware UI artifact.

63
00:04:13,287 --> 00:04:24,812
The callouts are the point: it shows the UI/demo surface, it should be quoted as a caption-bound hardware-validated artifact, and the on-screen telemetry should not be treated as a customer benchmark.

64
00:04:24,812 --> 00:04:26,662
Slide 11.

65
00:04:26,662 --> 00:04:28,412
Proof stack.

66
00:04:28,564 --> 00:04:30,939
Every proof has a label.

67
00:04:30,889 --> 00:04:37,214
Zynq Desk v0.39-K is hardware-validated as a current UI proof image.

68
00:04:37,214 --> 00:04:43,164
Linux userspace-to-FPGA is hardware-validated as a documented OS-to-bus path.

69
00:04:43,164 --> 00:04:48,439
The AX7020 matrix is live-measured and has wins and losses with caveats.

70
00:04:48,439 --> 00:04:55,164
Formal proof only applies to specifically audited properties; it does not imply workload or customer outcomes.

71
00:04:55,164 --> 00:04:59,514
Standalone boot is a build artifact until run proof promotes it.

72
00:04:59,514 --> 00:05:01,351
Slide 12.

73
00:05:01,351 --> 00:05:03,351
Commercial path.

74
00:05:03,351 --> 00:05:06,864
The commercial path starts in customer language.

75
00:05:06,864 --> 00:05:10,101
They show us the constrained part of the system.

76
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:14,364
We evaluate whether Atomik can help against the current baseline.

77
00:05:14,364 --> 00:05:19,926
If there is an opportunity, we show where it is, what was measured, and the potential impact.

78
00:05:19,926 --> 00:05:22,764
If there is no fit, we say so.

79
00:05:22,764 --> 00:05:28,589
Design partner work and licensing diligence only happen after measured proof supports the path.

80
00:05:28,589 --> 00:05:30,539
Slide 13.

81
00:05:30,539 --> 00:05:32,576
Financial model.

82
00:05:32,576 --> 00:05:36,264
This is founder-prepared planning, not a forecast.

83
00:05:36,264 --> 00:05:45,064
Planning ranges show how the business can move from proof reviews to paid evaluations, design-partner SOWs, and licensing only if proof converts.

84
00:05:45,064 --> 00:05:59,739
The use of funds is milestone-based: one point five million for engineering, one million for customer proof, seven hundred fifty thousand for IP and legal, seven hundred fifty thousand for ASIC feasibility, and one million for finance, GTM, ops, and reserve.

85
00:05:59,739 --> 00:06:01,689
Slide 14.

86
00:06:01,689 --> 00:06:03,739
Risks and gates.

87
00:06:03,739 --> 00:06:07,076
Trust comes from saying what is still unproven.

88
00:06:07,076 --> 00:06:09,814
Customer validation is pending.

89
00:06:09,814 --> 00:06:14,726
Battery, heat, water, footprint, and ROI need end-to-end measurement.

90
00:06:14,726 --> 00:06:19,189
ASIC economics need external feasibility review before tape-out.

91
00:06:19,189 --> 00:06:21,239
Incumbents are real.

92
00:06:21,239 --> 00:06:23,201
Slide 15.

93
00:06:23,201 --> 00:06:24,639
Team.

94
00:06:24,639 --> 00:06:35,914
Matt owns the deck, proof, roadmap, financial model, ask, and technical Q&A. Allison supports messaging, positioning, buyer-language, commercialization, and feedback where comfortable.

95
00:06:35,914 --> 00:06:43,401
Advisor priorities are ASIC/EDA feasibility, formal-methods review, and semiconductor IP/licensing.

96
00:06:43,401 --> 00:06:45,389
Slide 16.

97
00:06:45,389 --> 00:06:46,939
The ask.

98
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:53,064
The ask is five million dollars target pre-seed, with final SAFE terms CFO and counsel pending.

99
00:06:53,064 --> 00:06:59,839
The round buys measured workload proof, the IP packet, ASIC feasibility, and licensing-ready materials.

100
00:06:59,839 --> 00:07:05,476
The meeting-specific ask from Keaton is design-partner introductions and investor feedback.

101
00:07:05,476 --> 00:07:10,626
Keep Keaton-specific language in presenter notes, not in the circulating deck.

102
00:07:10,626 --> 00:07:12,126
Close.

103
00:07:12,126 --> 00:07:15,164
The next milestone is not a bigger claim.

104
00:07:15,164 --> 00:07:19,739
It is a measured customer workload artifact with correctness preserved.

105
00:07:19,739 --> 00:07:29,089
Atomik is asking the right customers to bring one constrained state path, measure the waste, and decide with evidence whether state-aware compute belongs in their architecture.

106
00:07:29,089 --> 00:07:31,264
Q&A guardrails.

107
00:07:31,264 --> 00:07:43,289
If they ask what is proven, say hardware-backed primitive checks, Linux userspace-to-FPGA validation, a live Zynq UI artifact, and live-measured AX7020 matrix artifacts with caveats.

108
00:07:43,289 --> 00:07:55,051
If they ask what is not proven, say customer workload value, battery extension, heat reduction, cooling reduction, water savings, footprint reduction, production readiness, and universal speedup.

109
00:07:55,051 --> 00:08:00,739
If they ask for valuation cap, say final SAFE mechanics are CFO and counsel pending.

